In case a query molecule is contained in the database (spectral library) to search in, we can use any method of spectral comparison, such as peak counting or dot product, to identify the correct answer. We now want to capture the more challenging case of a search where the query molecule is not contained in the database. Varmuza et al. [43, 44] suggested to iterate over all molecules in the database as queries, sort all remaining database entries with respect to some similarity score, and then to evaluate the average chemical similarity for each query in the top k for \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
